#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55742b20-d2eb-52dd-93ba-2bea32a81289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55742b20-d2eb-52dd-93ba-2bea32a81289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266161} :answer-field/id #uuid "181d8716-3c0a-5aff-9c35-c6ef0ef17dc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcb5129b-3804-548c-a47a-d6b56b122986" :question/math-academy-id "q-343424" :question/problem "The concentration $C$ (in millimoles per liter) of a chemical in a reactor $t$ minutes after the reaction begins is modeled by the function $C(t)={t}^{3}+25$ for $0\\le t\\le 10.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the concentration is changing with respect to time at the moment when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is\n{{field-1}} millimoles per liter per minute." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the chemical's concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(t) & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3}+25 & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3} & =125 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the chemical's concentration is changing when $t=5$ minutes is equal to ${C}^{′}(5).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $C(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+25) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(5)=3(5)^{2}=75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the chemical's concentration when its concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is  $75$ millimoles per liter per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe2982f-a752-54b7-b0f4-c50b37c260b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe2982f-a752-54b7-b0f4-c50b37c260b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266168} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e113cfa-03c5-531e-9d48-c21ef10c1193" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce974f27-56cb-5d6d-a571-c1f4112d1075" :question/math-academy-id "q-343502" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953428/assets/5fc1aff711355a147abac33a513434c55575c552ab58f0164ebc87222530f371.png)\n\nThe volume of a cube-shaped container is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is changing with respect to the side length at the moment when the face diagonal of the cube is equal to $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}$ centimeters is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the face diagonal of the cube is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$, its side length $s$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ Now, the volume of a cube with side length $s$ is given by \n\n$$\nV=V(s)={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{s}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=10$ gives\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(10)=3(10)^{2}=300.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is increasing when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$ is $300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$"}]}
